1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,574
Sheep.

2
00:00:00,736 --> 00:00:01,635
Come closer.

3
00:00:01,922 --> 00:00:03,158
I've got a deal for you.

4
00:00:03,495 --> 00:00:04,556
A tiny company.

5
00:00:04,719 --> 00:00:05,667
Nobody's heard of it.

6
00:00:05,879 --> 00:00:07,752
The stock costs one receipt.

7
00:00:08,476 --> 00:00:09,299
Next month?

8
00:00:09,488 --> 00:00:09,874
Ten.

9
00:00:09,911 --> 00:00:10,586
Maybe fifty.

10
00:00:10,610 --> 00:00:12,521
I'm really not supposed to tell you this.

11
00:00:13,145 --> 00:00:14,119
But I like you.

12
00:00:14,181 --> 00:00:15,367
I don't like you.

13
00:00:15,991 --> 00:00:17,264
That was a pump and dump.

14
00:00:17,602 --> 00:00:20,735
And in the next few minutes, I'll show you exactly how it shears you.

15
00:00:21,072 --> 00:00:21,696
Bald.

16
00:00:22,033 --> 00:00:27,913
And at the end, I'll give you a three-second test that would have saved every single sheep in this video.

17
00:00:28,253 --> 00:00:30,038
Let me show you how it really works.

18
00:00:30,225 --> 00:00:31,662
Meet my favourite customer.

19
00:00:32,285 --> 00:00:32,847
Gary.

20
00:00:32,935 --> 00:00:33,659
Ring ring.

21
00:00:33,759 --> 00:00:34,858
Gary, my friend.

22
00:00:35,855 --> 00:00:37,266
You seem like a smart sheep.

23
00:00:37,366 --> 00:00:38,802
I don't call just anyone.

24
00:00:39,264 --> 00:00:40,612
I've got something special.

25
00:00:41,011 --> 00:00:42,248
But it's only for today.

26
00:00:42,385 --> 00:00:43,821
Half the meadow is already in.

27
00:00:43,946 --> 00:00:44,707
Start small.

28
00:00:44,756 --> 00:00:45,893
Just ten receipts.

29
00:00:46,430 --> 00:00:47,916
And Gary says yes.

30
00:00:48,103 --> 00:00:49,426
Of course he does.

31
00:00:49,766 --> 00:00:51,363
First, what is a stock?

32
00:00:51,551 --> 00:00:53,061
Imagine a grass shop.

33
00:00:53,524 --> 00:00:57,456
The owner wants to grow, so she sells tiny pieces of her shop.

34
00:00:58,304 --> 00:00:59,666
Each piece is a share.

35
00:01:00,065 --> 00:01:03,148
If the shop does well, your piece is worth more wool.

36
00:01:03,685 --> 00:01:06,119
If it does badly, your piece is worth less.

37
00:01:06,582 --> 00:01:07,181
Simple.

38
00:01:07,305 --> 00:01:10,626
Now, big famous companies have millions of owners.

39
00:01:10,963 --> 00:01:15,995
Millions of buyers, millions of sellers, and a lot of very boring people watching every move.

40
00:01:16,843 --> 00:01:19,328
Pushing their price around is hard.

41
00:01:19,671 --> 00:01:21,418
But some companies are tiny.

42
00:01:21,818 --> 00:01:24,914
A shop with three bundles of grass and a dream.

43
00:01:25,639 --> 00:01:27,361
Their shares cost pennies.

44
00:01:27,760 --> 00:01:29,558
We call them penny stocks.

45
00:01:29,896 --> 00:01:31,256
Barely anyone buys them.

46
00:01:31,393 --> 00:01:32,941
Barely anyone watches them.

47
00:01:33,564 --> 00:01:38,845
And with a stock that small, a few big buyers can push the price almost anywhere they want.

48
00:01:39,570 --> 00:01:41,854
So, can you guess who has the big buyers?

49
00:01:42,478 --> 00:01:43,240
Hi.

50
00:01:43,579 --> 00:01:44,378
Step one.

51
00:01:45,377 --> 00:01:47,574
I buy a mountain of Miracle Grass.

52
00:01:48,198 --> 00:01:48,860
Quietly.

53
00:01:48,947 --> 00:01:50,070
One receipt per share.

54
00:01:50,157 --> 00:01:50,994
Nobody notices.

55
00:01:51,032 --> 00:01:51,843
Nobody cares.

56
00:01:51,943 --> 00:01:53,166
Yet.

57
00:01:53,497 --> 00:01:54,359
Step two.

58
00:01:54,546 --> 00:01:58,928
I hire a room full of very charming wolves with very loud telephones.

59
00:01:58,978 --> 00:02:00,289
They call it a boiler room.

60
00:02:00,476 --> 00:02:01,487
And they call sheep.

61
00:02:01,575 --> 00:02:02,661
Thousands of sheep.

62
00:02:02,723 --> 00:02:03,447
All day long.

63
00:02:03,472 --> 00:02:04,308
They have a script.

64
00:02:04,383 --> 00:02:05,507
Once in a lifetime.

65
00:02:05,694 --> 00:02:06,531
Only today.

66
00:02:06,743 --> 00:02:07,991
Everyone's already buying.

67
00:02:08,240 --> 00:02:10,414
They never say: this might be a terrible idea.

68
00:02:10,812 --> 00:02:12,398
They say: you'll miss out.

69
00:02:12,586 --> 00:02:14,171
But that's not the worst part.

70
00:02:15,169 --> 00:02:16,518
Some sheep say no.

71
00:02:16,917 --> 00:02:18,129
So we send them news.

72
00:02:18,265 --> 00:02:19,489
Amazing news.

73
00:02:19,889 --> 00:02:21,187
That we wrote ourselves.

74
00:02:21,523 --> 00:02:23,208
And sheep do what sheep do best.

75
00:02:23,259 --> 00:02:24,482
They follow the other sheep.

76
00:02:24,519 --> 00:02:25,531
Everybody buys.

77
00:02:25,568 --> 00:02:26,654
The price goes up.

78
00:02:26,841 --> 00:02:28,290
Which makes more sheep buy.

79
00:02:28,415 --> 00:02:30,437
Which makes the price go up even more.

80
00:02:30,836 --> 00:02:31,560
It's beautiful.

81
00:02:31,586 --> 00:02:34,494
It's like watching wool grow in fast forward.

82
00:02:34,841 --> 00:02:37,300
Let's break down exactly what I did to Gary.

83
00:02:37,638 --> 00:02:38,698
Trick number one.

84
00:02:39,035 --> 00:02:39,697
Flattery.

85
00:02:39,759 --> 00:02:42,443
A sheep who feels smart stops asking questions.

86
00:02:42,656 --> 00:02:43,617
Trick number two.

87
00:02:43,804 --> 00:02:44,678
A deadline.

88
00:02:45,215 --> 00:02:47,986
No time to think means no time to say no.

89
00:02:48,323 --> 00:02:49,334
Trick number three.

90
00:02:49,584 --> 00:02:50,870
Everyone else is doing it.

91
00:02:51,206 --> 00:02:53,603
Sheep trust sheep more than they trust facts.

92
00:02:54,228 --> 00:02:55,263
Trick number four.

93
00:02:55,389 --> 00:02:56,387
Start small.

94
00:02:56,725 --> 00:02:58,347
Ten receipts feels safe.

95
00:02:58,810 --> 00:03:01,605
Next week, I'll call again, and ask for a hundred.

96
00:03:02,604 --> 00:03:03,378
Four tricks.

97
00:03:03,441 --> 00:03:04,502
One phone call.

98
00:03:05,039 --> 00:03:06,849
Gary never had a chance.

99
00:03:07,187 --> 00:03:08,360
And then, step three.

100
00:03:08,485 --> 00:03:11,744
At the very top, I sell everything I bought at one receipt.

101
00:03:12,368 --> 00:03:13,267
For fifteen.

102
00:03:13,454 --> 00:03:15,002
Now there's nobody left to buy.

103
00:03:15,339 --> 00:03:16,625
Because I was the buyer.

104
00:03:16,838 --> 00:03:18,460
The price falls off a cliff.

105
00:03:18,498 --> 00:03:21,968
And the sheep are holding a very expensive piece of nothing.

106
00:03:22,217 --> 00:03:23,841
Wolves walk away fluffy.

107
00:03:24,128 --> 00:03:25,488
Sheep walk away bald.

108
00:03:25,888 --> 00:03:27,161
Every single time.

109
00:03:27,497 --> 00:03:30,444
But why do sheep fall for it, again and again?

110
00:03:31,067 --> 00:03:31,966
It has a name.

111
00:03:32,054 --> 00:03:33,190
Fear of missing out.

112
00:03:33,590 --> 00:03:37,759
When the sheep next to you gets rich, your brain doesn't see a graph.

113
00:03:38,383 --> 00:03:40,568
It sees a sheep with more wool than you.

114
00:03:41,192 --> 00:03:43,939
And that hurts more than losing.

115
00:03:44,126 --> 00:03:45,462
So you buy at the top.

116
00:03:45,862 --> 00:03:47,647
Right when the wolves are selling.

117
00:03:48,270 --> 00:03:49,545
Greed brings you in.

118
00:03:50,168 --> 00:03:51,879
Fear makes you sell at the bottom.

119
00:03:52,503 --> 00:03:53,802
Buy high, sell low.

120
00:03:53,926 --> 00:03:56,310
The sheep's favourite strategy.

121
00:03:56,650 --> 00:03:57,736
You know what's funny?

122
00:03:58,074 --> 00:04:00,121
I didn't even need to lie about the company.

123
00:04:00,408 --> 00:04:02,267
I just needed you to stop thinking.

124
00:04:02,667 --> 00:04:05,127
Every pump in history works the same way.

125
00:04:05,751 --> 00:04:06,962
Excitement goes up.

126
00:04:07,299 --> 00:04:08,596
Thinking goes down.

127
00:04:09,221 --> 00:04:12,530
And somewhere in the middle, a wolf gets very fluffy.

128
00:04:12,867 --> 00:04:14,128
And this is not new.

129
00:04:14,253 --> 00:04:15,277
Not even close.

130
00:04:15,613 --> 00:04:17,436
London, seventeen twenty.

131
00:04:17,972 --> 00:04:22,080
The South Sea Company promised huge profits from trade with South America.

132
00:04:22,329 --> 00:04:23,453
Everyone wanted in.

133
00:04:23,791 --> 00:04:29,621
Shares that cost around one hundred pounds at the start of the year went to around one thousand by the summer.

134
00:04:30,245 --> 00:04:31,655
Then the bubble popped.

135
00:04:31,993 --> 00:04:34,539
By the end of the year, most of that was gone.

136
00:04:35,164 --> 00:04:38,148
Even Isaac Newton reportedly lost a fortune.

137
00:04:38,771 --> 00:04:40,220
The smartest man in England.

138
00:04:40,620 --> 00:04:41,356
Fleeced.

139
00:04:42,354 --> 00:04:44,714
Three hundred years later, the costumes changed.

140
00:04:44,739 --> 00:04:46,324
The trick didn't.

141
00:04:46,660 --> 00:04:49,306
Now you're thinking, that's just a cartoon about sheep.

142
00:04:49,769 --> 00:04:50,543
It's not.

143
00:04:51,167 --> 00:04:56,697
In nineteen eighty-nine, on Long Island, New York, a brokerage firm opened called Stratton Oakmont.

144
00:04:57,234 --> 00:05:00,281
It was run by a young broker named Jordan Belfort.

145
00:05:00,904 --> 00:05:06,747
Rooms full of young brokers, phones glued to their ears, pushing tiny stocks to ordinary people.

146
00:05:06,997 --> 00:05:07,983
Pump the price.

147
00:05:08,195 --> 00:05:09,282
Dump the shares.

148
00:05:09,531 --> 00:05:10,205
Repeat.

149
00:05:10,829 --> 00:05:13,938
Regulators shut the firm down in nineteen ninety-six.

150
00:05:14,274 --> 00:05:18,057
Belfort later pleaded guilty to securities fraud and money laundering.

151
00:05:18,594 --> 00:05:20,641
He spent about twenty-two months in prison.

152
00:05:21,366 --> 00:05:26,184
And a court ordered him to pay back about one hundred and ten million dollars to his victims.

153
00:05:26,909 --> 00:05:29,455
His story later became a famous Hollywood movie.

154
00:05:30,080 --> 00:05:30,990
Great movie.

155
00:05:31,615 --> 00:05:34,399
Terrible retirement plan for the investors.

156
00:05:34,735 --> 00:05:36,308
So, is this legal?

157
00:05:36,470 --> 00:05:36,958
No.

158
00:05:37,494 --> 00:05:43,436
Hyping a stock with lies so you can dump it on other people is market manipulation and fraud.

159
00:05:43,973 --> 00:05:46,133
Regulators like the SEC chase it.

160
00:05:46,756 --> 00:05:49,540
But they usually arrive after the sheep are already bald.

161
00:05:50,003 --> 00:05:51,189
Justice is slow.

162
00:05:51,376 --> 00:05:53,710
Wool grows back even slower.

163
00:05:54,047 --> 00:05:55,171
But here's the thing.

164
00:05:55,383 --> 00:05:57,405
Pump and dumps didn't disappear.

165
00:05:58,255 --> 00:05:59,853
They just changed phones.

166
00:06:00,140 --> 00:06:02,624
Today, it's not a loud broker calling your house.

167
00:06:02,961 --> 00:06:04,134
It's a group chat.

168
00:06:04,758 --> 00:06:07,230
An influencer with expensive sunglasses.

169
00:06:07,693 --> 00:06:09,989
A random account full of rocket emojis.

170
00:06:10,326 --> 00:06:12,424
A brand new coin with a funny name.

171
00:06:12,885 --> 00:06:13,710
Same trick.

172
00:06:14,433 --> 00:06:16,443
In twenty twenty-two, the U.S.

173
00:06:16,468 --> 00:06:24,520
Securities and Exchange Commission charged eight social media influencers in a pump and dump scheme worth about one hundred million dollars.

174
00:06:25,369 --> 00:06:26,417
Different costume.

175
00:06:27,142 --> 00:06:27,966
Same wolf.

176
00:06:28,428 --> 00:06:29,726
And here's the worst part.

177
00:06:29,938 --> 00:06:33,396
The loudest voice in the chat is usually the one who bought first.

178
00:06:33,858 --> 00:06:35,331
They don't need you to get rich.

179
00:06:36,055 --> 00:06:38,689
They need you to be the exit.

180
00:06:39,039 --> 00:06:40,762
There's an old Wall Street legend.

181
00:06:40,887 --> 00:06:42,410
New York, nineteen twenty-nine.

182
00:06:42,547 --> 00:06:45,493
A rich investor stops to get his shoes shined.

183
00:06:46,031 --> 00:06:48,402
And the shoeshine boy gives him a stock tip.

184
00:06:48,939 --> 00:06:53,733
The investor thinks: if even the shoeshine boy is buying, who's left to buy?

185
00:06:54,271 --> 00:06:55,544
So he sells everything.

186
00:06:55,731 --> 00:06:57,529
Weeks later, the market crashes.

187
00:06:57,990 --> 00:06:59,801
Nobody knows if the story is true.

188
00:07:00,424 --> 00:07:01,473
But the lesson is.

189
00:07:01,548 --> 00:07:05,805
When everyone is shouting buy, the wolves are already selling.

190
00:07:06,143 --> 00:07:08,802
Here's how a modern pump looks, step by step.

191
00:07:08,902 --> 00:07:10,925
Someone creates a coin in five minutes.

192
00:07:11,174 --> 00:07:13,047
They keep a big pile for themselves.

193
00:07:13,259 --> 00:07:15,020
They pay accounts to shout about it.

194
00:07:15,144 --> 00:07:16,106
Sheep rush in.

195
00:07:16,230 --> 00:07:17,604
The price goes vertical.

196
00:07:17,704 --> 00:07:18,852
The creators sell.

197
00:07:19,388 --> 00:07:21,636
Sometimes, the whole thing takes a few hours.

198
00:07:21,798 --> 00:07:25,269
By the time Gary hears about it, it's already over.

199
00:07:25,611 --> 00:07:27,796
And if you already bought something like this?

200
00:07:28,045 --> 00:07:31,004
Don't let the same chat talk you into buying more to win it back.

201
00:07:31,466 --> 00:07:34,050
That's how one haircut becomes two.

202
00:07:34,392 --> 00:07:36,926
So how do you tell a real company from a pump?

203
00:07:37,463 --> 00:07:39,735
A real company grows wool slowly.

204
00:07:39,947 --> 00:07:41,869
It sells more grass every year.

205
00:07:42,156 --> 00:07:46,377
Its price goes up because the business grows, not because someone shouts.

206
00:07:47,100 --> 00:07:48,498
A pump has a story.

207
00:07:49,223 --> 00:07:50,009
No business.

208
00:07:50,734 --> 00:07:52,530
So ask two boring questions.

209
00:07:52,781 --> 00:07:54,866
What does this company actually sell?

210
00:07:55,202 --> 00:07:56,426
And does it make any money?

211
00:07:56,887 --> 00:07:59,709
If the answer is vibes, you know what to do.

212
00:08:00,049 --> 00:08:00,848
Alright.

213
00:08:01,385 --> 00:08:02,671
I promised you a test.

214
00:08:02,733 --> 00:08:03,507
Here it is.

215
00:08:04,231 --> 00:08:07,839
If someone you don't know is in a hurry to make you rich, ask one question.

216
00:08:08,688 --> 00:08:09,923
Why are they telling me?

217
00:08:10,548 --> 00:08:13,594
If it's so good, why aren't they buying quietly, like I did?

218
00:08:14,219 --> 00:08:14,955
That's it.

219
00:08:15,354 --> 00:08:16,141
Three seconds.

220
00:08:16,166 --> 00:08:17,452
And three red flags.

221
00:08:17,489 --> 00:08:17,926
One.

222
00:08:18,114 --> 00:08:19,898
A tiny company nobody has heard of.

223
00:08:20,298 --> 00:08:20,773
Two.

224
00:08:20,935 --> 00:08:21,983
Huge promises.

225
00:08:22,021 --> 00:08:22,520
Three.

226
00:08:22,645 --> 00:08:23,419
Buy right now.

227
00:08:23,444 --> 00:08:24,480
All three together?

228
00:08:25,104 --> 00:08:25,929
That's a wolf.

229
00:08:26,029 --> 00:08:26,765
Every time.

230
00:08:27,226 --> 00:08:28,575
Not financial advice.

231
00:08:29,037 --> 00:08:30,510
I'm a wolf.

232
00:08:30,843 --> 00:08:31,542
Okay.

233
00:08:31,618 --> 00:08:33,202
Let's put it all in one place.

234
00:08:33,365 --> 00:08:34,090
Four tricks.

235
00:08:34,139 --> 00:08:34,800
Flattery.

236
00:08:34,851 --> 00:08:35,712
A deadline.

237
00:08:35,812 --> 00:08:36,486
The herd.

238
00:08:36,673 --> 00:08:37,548
Start small.

239
00:08:37,647 --> 00:08:38,445
One test.

240
00:08:38,658 --> 00:08:39,744
Why are they telling me?

241
00:08:40,368 --> 00:08:41,454
Screenshot this.

242
00:08:41,667 --> 00:08:43,726
Send it to the Gary in your life.

243
00:08:44,065 --> 00:08:44,815
And Gary?

244
00:08:44,914 --> 00:08:45,739
Gary's fine.

245
00:08:46,075 --> 00:08:46,612
Bald.

246
00:08:46,700 --> 00:08:47,536
But fine.

247
00:08:48,160 --> 00:08:50,007
And this morning, his phone rang again.

248
00:08:50,352 --> 00:08:51,738
So, that's a pump and dump.

249
00:08:51,925 --> 00:08:52,686
Excuse me.

250
00:08:52,762 --> 00:08:53,561
It's a sheep.

251
00:08:54,184 --> 00:08:56,082
Wants to know about a hot new stock.

252
00:08:56,182 --> 00:08:56,919
Hello, sheep.

253
00:08:57,642 --> 00:09:01,076
If you don't want to end up bald like Gary… subscribe.

254
00:09:01,699 --> 00:09:03,435
Gary bought Miracle Grass.
